DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ttyyppoo looks for likely typographical errors in an English-
     language document by a statistical test, rather than by
     matching words against a fixed dictionary.  For each word,
     it computes an index of peculiarity from the digram (letter-
     pair) and trigram (letter-triple) frequencies of that word,
     measured against the digram and trigram frequencies of the
     whole document (helped, unless -nn is given, by built-in fre-
     quency tables and a small exception dictionary of common
     technical words).  Words that use letter combinations rarely
     seen elsewhere in the document score a high index and are
     the ones most likely to be misspellings.

     ttyyppoo reads each _f_i_l_e named (or the standard input if none is
     given), extracts the alphabetic words, folds them to lower
     case, and discards any word found in its exception diction-
     ary (/uussrr/ddiicctt/ddiicctt).  It sorts the remaining distinct words
     by descending index of peculiarity and prints each one
     preceded by its index.

     -nn   Do not load the built-in digram and trigram statistics
          or the exception dictionary.  More words will be
          printed, and their indices of peculiarity will be less
          reliable, especially for a short document.

     -rr   Raw mode: do not strip lines beginning with `.' or
          remove the nnrrooffff escape character `\', which ttyyppoo
          otherwise assumes surround formatting requests rather
          than text.

     -ss   Instead of printing an index of peculiarity for each
          word, write the digram and trigram frequency counts ob-
          served in the input to files named ddiiggrraammss and ttrriiggrraammss
          in the current directory.  These are the same format as
          /uussrr/ddiicctt/ddiiggrraammss and /uussrr/ddiicctt/ttrriiggrraammss and are meant
          for regenerating the built-in tables, not for everyday
          use.

